(1080 BC to 1074 BC) during the reign of Pharaoh Ramesses XI.", "newValue": "Herihor, a formidable figure in the late 11th century BCE (fl. c. 1080–1074 BCE), stands as a pivotal architect in the fragmentation of Egypt's centralized authority, marking the transition from the unified New Kingdom to the fractured Third Intermediate Period. Rising from a military commander and vizier under the 20th Dynasty pharaoh Ramesses XI, Herihor ultimately seized de facto control of Upper Egypt, consolidating immense power as the High Priest of Amun at Thebes. His ascent, concurrent with the profound decline of the pharaonic institution, created a dual monarchy, with Herihor and his successors ruling the south from Thebes, while weak pharaohs nominally controlled the Delta, irrevocably altering the geopolitical landscape of ancient Egypt.

His career trajectory saw him accumulate an array of powerful titles: 'Overseer of the Granaries,' 'Commander of the Army,' and 'Vizier,' before his decisive move to claim the supreme religious office of High Priest of Amun at Karnak. This position granted him control over the immense wealth and landholdings of the Amun priesthood, effectively making him the wealthiest and most influential man in Upper Egypt. By approximately 1080 BCE, Herihor had begun to adopt royal prerogatives, including inscribing his name in cartouches and commissioning building projects, effectively operating as an independent monarch. He initiated the restoration of royal tombs in the Valley of the Kings, a testament to his assumption of traditional pharaonic duties, and his authority extended over Nubia, maintaining Egypt's southern frontier during a period of internal upheaval."}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-10T07:47:42Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27261013137", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1200 BCE"}, {"title": "Spiritual seeking and religious community formation", "type": "Idea", "year": "1200 BCE"}], "newValue": ["The prolonged economic decline and political instability of the late New Kingdom weakened the centralized authority of the pharaoh.", "The immense wealth and landholdings of the Amun priesthood in Thebes provided a powerful independent power base.", "The inability of Pharaoh Ramesses XI to assert effective control over Upper Egypt created a power vacuum that ambitious officials could exploit."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-10T07:47:42Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27261013137", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1080 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping su